Very few games are truly descended from rogue or hack.  Many were
inspired by rogue and hack and their direct descendants (nethack,
advrogue), however.

ADOM was built from scratch, inspired by Nethack and Omega among others.
Omega was built from scratch, doubtless inspired by rogue and (Net)hack.
Linley's Dungeon Crawl was built from scratch with inspiration from
various places, and is the single most successful C++ roguelike.  Larn
was built from scratch, and I believe it may have been reimplemented on
occasion.

Moria was built from scratch by Robert Koeneke because he'd played Rogue
on a Unix system and then found himself in a VMS environment; a guy
called James Wilson reimplemented Moria in C on UNIX instead of Pascal
on VMS, and UMoria (as this version was known) later begat Angband.

Dare I mention the shareware Windows game "Castle of the Winds"?  That
was a reasonable approximation to a roguelike, as are (in some respects
but not others) Blizzard's Diablo and Diablo II, SSI's "Dungeon Hack",
...

Each of these games plays differently to the others.

And if you go over to rgr.misc and rgr.development, you will hear tell
of many other roguelikes, some of which are a long way indeed from rogue
itself.  Alphaman.  GearHead.  Abura Tan.  Avanor.

